.TH WAIT 2
.SH NAME
wait \- wait for process to terminate
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/wait.h>
.PP
.B int wait(int *status);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Wait
causes its caller to delay until a signal is received or
one of its child
processes terminates.
If any child has died since the last
.I wait,
return is immediate;
if there are no children, return is immediate with
the error bit set
(resp. with a value of \-1 returned).
The normal return yields the process ID of the terminated child.
In the case of several children several
.I wait
calls are needed
to learn of all the deaths.
.PP
If
.RI (int) status
is nonzero, the high byte of the word pointed to
receives the low byte of the
argument of
.I exit
when the child terminated.
The low byte
receives the termination status
of the process.
See
.IR signal (2)
for a list of termination statuses (signals);
0 status indicates normal termination.
A special status (0177) is returned for a stopped process
which has not terminated and can be restarted.
See
.IR ptrace (2).
If the 0200 bit of the termination status
is set,
a core image of the process was produced
by the system.
.PP
If the parent process terminates without
waiting on its children,
the initialization process
(process ID = 1)
inherits the children.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
exit(2), fork(2), signal(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Returns
\-1 if there are no children not previously waited for.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS The status does not always fit
The gate takes no arguments at all: the process id comes back in the accumulator
and
.B the status in r12,
which the stub stores through the caller's pointer.
.PP
.B r12 is an index register \- 15 bits \-
and a wait status is (code << 8), which passes 32767 as soon as the exit code
passes 127.
.B Exit codes 128..255 therefore arrive truncated,
identically here and under b6sim.  Widening it means giving the gate an argument
again and writing the status through the caller's pointer on the kernel side;
until then, exit codes should stay small.
.PP
That second-result convention is what makes this stub hand-written rather than
generated, and it is shared with
.IR fork (2),
.IR pipe (2),
.IR getpid (2)
and
.IR getuid (2).
See
.IR intro (2).
.SH ASSEMBLER
.B Note:
v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here \- a decimal call number and a
`\fBsys\fP \fIname\fP; \fIarg\fP ...' line.  Neither is how a call is made on this
machine.  The gate is one extracode \-
.B $77 SYS_wait
\- and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n\-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.
.PP
.B The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.
The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is the only place a syscall number
appears on the user side.  See
.IR intro (2)
for the whole contract.
